  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Drought Emergency Response Project in Marsabit, Phase II</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Marsabit County is experiencing one of the most severe drought never experienced before, as a result of failure of 5 consecutive rain seasons resulting to extreme water shortage, food insecurity, migration of pastoral communities, resource based conflicts,  malnutrition etc.The situation has been excerbated by high cost of food prices making it beyond reach for many and lack of market for the weak livestock. Currently, water trucking and food assistance (through food distribution, cash transfers and food vouchers) to the most affected people and institutions are amongst some of the drought emergency interventions supported by CAFOD, the National and County Government and all partners working in the humanitarian aid relief. Unless supported through interventions such as water trucking and food donations most people and institutions currently do not have access to safe, clean drinking water and food.
In the ongoing 3rd Phase of CAFOD GEF funding &amp; support from Caritas Australia, the project targets 7 severely drought affected villages with food distribution, food voucher and water trucking, 5 schools with school feeding programs, 40 needy students with food for fees, construction of an underground tank in Tullu Dimtu and borehole drilling in Burgabo. The project is also targeting 11 strategic diesel-run boreholes among drought-affected communities of Marsabit County with the provision of fuel subsidies and 10 boreholes with fast moving spare parts. Additional funding from CAFOD GEF will support 3 villages with food distribution, water trucking, solar relocation in Burgabo and Genset house and school feeding programme for 1 school, and food for fees for 40 students. The direct beneficiaries will include school going children, men, women, pregnant and lactating women, People Living with Disability and youths in these villages. The vulnerable section of the community such as poor, elderly, female-headed households, people living with chronic diseases.</narrative>
  </description>
